At the time of Mesogean arrival, the territory of modern-day western Balisca had as many as 2,000 tribes. The Indigenous peoples were traditionally mostly semi-nomadic tribes that subsisted on hunting, fishing, gathering, and migrant agriculture. When the Volisanians arrived in 1500, the Natives lived mainly on the coast and along the banks of major rivers, with "the earliest going back at least 10,000 years in the highlands of [[Pindorama]]". The dating of the origins of the first inhabitants, who were called "[[W:Patagons|Patagons]]" (''patagón'') by the Mesogeans, is still a matter of dispute among archaeologists. The earliest pottery ever found in Balisca, radiocarbon-dated 8,000 years old, has been excavated in the territory of the former [[Tapuya Confederacy]], near [[Yvyra'ara]], providing evidence to overturn the assumption that the tropical forest region was too poor in resources to have supported a complex prehistoric culture. 

The current most widely accepted view of anthropologists, linguists and geneticists is that the early tribes were part of the first wave of [[W:Nomad|nomadic]] [[W:Hunter-gatherer|hunter-gatherers]] who came into the Algarves from [[Fosia]], either by land, across the [[W:Bering Strait|x Strait]], or by sea routes across the [[Shendan Ocean]], or both.

The formative period of [[Basabyala]] is one of the [[TBA#]] independent [[W:Cradles of civilization|cradles of civilization]], this era saw the origin of distinct cultural traits such as religious and symbolic traditions, [[W:Potato|potato]] and [[W:Quinoa|quinoa]] cultivation, artistic and architectural complexes that spread from [[Matarrañyan civilizations|Matarrañyan cultures]] to the rest of the [[Marañón basin]]. Villages became more densely populated, socially stratified with an artisan class, and developed into chiefdoms. The most powerful rulers had religious and political power, organizing the construction of ceremonial centers.

The [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] on the east coast of Abyala created a rather sharp cultural boundary between the settled agrarian civilizations of the east coast and the semi-nomadic tribes of the west, who never developed written records or permanent monumental architecture. Archaeological remains (mainly pottery) indicate a complex pattern of regional cultural developments, internal migrations, and occasional large [[W:Confederation#Indigenous confederations in South America|state-like confederations]]. This included the aforementioned [[Tapuya]], and the [[Tapajóara culture]] (River Alliance), which came to control much of the Lower [[Marañón River]] [[Marañón basin|basin]], through a combination of agricultural innovation, riverine trade networks, and military conquest.
[[File:80 - Machu Picchu - Juin 2009 - crop.jpg|left|thumb|[[Itá-Marangatu]] ({{Literal translation|sacred stone}}) was a religious site and [[W:Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]] destination of the [[Aruanãyára Empire#Religion|Aruanãyára]]]]
In the 15th century, the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára emerged as a powerful state]] which, in the span of a century, formed one of the largest empire in the [[pre-colonial Abyala]] with their capital in [[Takuakesetyba|Takûakesétyba]]. This group represented one of the subgroups of the wider [[Kurupiranga|Kurupiranga ethnic groups]], which had dispersed throughout the [[Marañón basin]]. Gradually, as early as the thirteenth century, they began to expand and incorporate their neighbors. Aruanãyáran expansion was slow until about the middle of the fifteenth century, when the pace of conquest began to accelerate, particularly under the rule of the emperor [[TBA]]. The Aruanãyárans came to control most of the [[Matarrañya|Southern Matarrañyan]] region, ruling over a population of 5 to 10 million inhabitants. 

From 1460 to 1520, the Aruanãyára Empire used a variety of methods, from violent conquest to peaceful assimilation, to incorporate a large portion of eastern Abyala, centered on the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], from northern [[Lacashe]] to the southern Alpujarra, between the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] in the west and the [[Marañón rainforest]]  in the east. The official language of the empire was [[Classical Aruanãyára]], although hundreds of local languages and dialects were spoken. They referred to their empire as ''Itá-Rapé-Retã'' which can be translated as "Land of the Stone Path." Many local forms of worship persisted in the empire, most of them concerning local sacred spirits, but the Aruanãyára leadership encouraged the worship of [[W:Tupã|''Tupã'']], manifestation of god through thunder, and imposed its sovereignty above other cults such as that of [[W:Jurupari|Jurupari]]. The Aruanãyára [[W:Imperial cult|considered their leader]] to be ''Rupave'' "father of the people" to be a [[W:Demigod|demigod]].

Troops of the Aruanãyára Empire are reported to have reached [[Alagón River|Alagón river]] and fought with the local [[Kuarupíba]]. Thus, the northern border of the Empire is believed by most modern scholars to be situated somewhere between [[Castejón]] and the [[Akûtukû Desert]].

The [[W:Mapuche|Wallche]] inhabited the northern regions of Tinguiririca, particularly around present-day [[Cadaqués]] and [[Dríadas]]. Their society was agricultural, cultivating [[W:Maize|maize]], [[W:Beans|beans]], [[W:Squash|squash]], and [[W:Potatoes|potatoes]], and to a lesser extent practiced hunting and fishing. They developed advanced farming techniques, including terracing and irrigation, allowing them to sustain large populations. The Wallche also engaged in sophisticated forms of social organization, with each village, or ''lof'', led by a chief, or ''lonko''. They practiced a [[W:Mapuche religion|polytheistic religion]] with a rich mythology, deeply connected to the natural world. In contrast, the [[W:Yaghan people|Yamana people]] lived a nomadic lifestyle as hunter-gatherers in the southern [[W:Fjord|fjords]] and [[W:Ria|rias]]. They were skilled mariners, using [[W:Dugout canoe|dugout canoes]] and [[W:Dalca|dalcas]]—boats made from planks sewn together with natural fibers—to navigate the complex waterways. The Yamna relied on fishing, hunting sea mammals, and gathering shellfish and wild plants. Their communities were small and highly mobile, adapting to the harsh, variable climate of southern Cerdanya. The Yamana had a profound spiritual connection to the sea and their environment, with intricate rituals and oral traditions passed down through generations. 

[[File:Square rigged caravels.jpg|thumb|The [[W:Square-rigged caravel|square-rigged caravels]] of [[Xenokrates Lizoain]]'s armada of 1500 that landed at [[Baía de Vera Creu]]]]The first Illypnian to step foot in what is now Balisca arrived on 2 June 1500, with the arrival of an [[Crown of Aragon|Aragonese]] fleet commanded by the [[Bizkarra|Bizkarran]] explorer [[Xenokrates Lizoain]] at [[Baía de Vera Creu]] (within present-day [[Piedmont]]). The explorers first encountered the Guanarú tribe, which spoke a dialect of the [[W:Tupi–Guarani languages|Kurupiran-Nyengatú]] languages. The initial encounter between the settlers and the indigenous tribes was a delicate balance of curiosity and caution, with the natives welcoming Lizoain and his crew with a [[W:Tearful salutation|tearful salutation]]. Upon sailing around the [[Tinguirirican Peninsula]] and discovering the [[Marañón Delta]], Lizoain named the entire land ''Patagoi basilika'' (or "Balisca of the [[W:Patagons|Patagons]]"), in reference to height of the natives (presumed to be [[W:Yaghan people|Yamana]]) he encountered in the north, and the [[W:Acai berries|acai berries]] which had a similar appearance to that of the [[W:History of La Rioja wine|bygone ancient grape vines]] from his homeland. Lizoain maintained a cordial relationship with native leaders, especially that of the [[Tapajóara culture|Tapajóara peoples]] originating from [[Pindorama]] and the skilled [[W:Caiçara|Caiçara]] sailors, whom he viewed as instrumental in the exploration of the [[Marañón basin|Marañón]]. 

=== Early Illypnian colonization and conflict ===
{{Main|Great Disappointment|Colonization of Balisca|Viceroyalty of New Volisania}}

[[File:Los tres mulatos de Esmeraldas (Sánchez Galque).jpg|left|thumb|320x320px|The ''Black Kings of'' ''Abyala'' by [[W:Es:Andrés Sánchez Gallque|Andrés Sánchez Gallque]], depicting [[Colonial history of Burawa|Burawi]] explorers from [[Dakare]] (then part of the [[Empire of Burawa]]) in [[Lacashe]].]]
At the end of the 15th century, Abyala was estimated to have been home to between thirty and forty million indigenous people in what was one of the largest indigenous populations in the Western Hemisphere. Although not without conflict, the colonist's early interactions with the [[Kurupiranga people|Kurupiranga]] peoples and smaller indigenous populations were relatively peaceful. However repeated outbreaks of {{W|European infectious diseases|Old World infectious diseases}} such as {{W|influenza}}, {{W|measles}} and {{W|smallpox}} (to which they had no natural immunity), combined with other effects of colonial contact, resulted in a twenty-five percent to sixty percent Indigenous population decrease post-contact. 
[[File:Ruínas 1.jpg|thumb|The ruins of the Cathar mission of [[Sant Domènec de les Missions]], [[Estuaria]]]]
In the newly explored coastal areas, the Volisanian-chartered fleets (consisting of an ethnically diverse crew from around the [[Mesogean Sea]]) adopted a strategy of establishing small coastal settlements called [[W:Factory (trading post)|factories]] ({{Langx|pml|fabrica}}) from which they engaged in trade with the Indigenous peoples. Alongside these trading outposts, Christian and Muslim missionaries also practiced gathering native populations into communities called "[[W:Reductions|reductions]]" ({{Lang-an|recollidas  }}, {{Langx|pml|reducions}}, {{Langx|ca|reduccions}}). The objectives of the reductions were to impart their faith and Mesogean culture. Secular as well as religious authorities created "reductions". The missions established by the [[Cathars]] were unique in that they were almost universally secular and remained autonomous and isolated from other Mesogean colonists and rule. The Cathars ultimately created a "[[W:State within a state|state within a state]]", and a major factor attracting the natives to the Cathars reductions was the protection they afforded from enslavement and the forced labour of ''[[W:Encomienda|comanadoria]]'' and the ability to continue their traditional lifestyles relatively unrestricted.

Cathars strongly condemned the forcing of natives into {{W|slavery#chattel slavery|chattel slavery}} across the territory. Similar actions were not taken by the remainder of the [[Hesperian League]], whose members were already heavily involved in the {{W|slave trade}} on the Mesogean. To satisfy the demand for labor, the Mesogean colonies turned to the [[Aethiopian slave trade]] (part of the larger [[Kaftian slave trade]]), as suggested by prominent reformists such as {{W|Bartolomé de las Casas}}. In 1533 the first ship carrying Kaftian slaves arrived in Balisca, at [[Corpus Christi]]. The importation of Kaftian slaves rapidly picked up the pace as Mesogean colonists increased production of [[W:Brazilwood|brazilwood]] and [[W:Sugarcane|sugarcane]] to satisfy increasing demand in Illypnia. By 1537, the native population of Balisca was estimated to number under five million people, down from an all time high of around 18 million prior to the arrival of the Mesogeans. Tales of the widespread suffering and exploitation of the Indigenous people at the hands of the Illypnians were spread by the [[Catharism|Catharist community]], giving rise to the [[Black Legend (Abyala)|Black Legend]]. Furthermore, to ensure peace and respect between the settlers and the indigenous peoples, the mandated that settlers not interfere with populated native areas, and set out to demarcate divisions between the two groups. This resulted in the prohibition of Illypnian expansion into the interior of the island of [[Pindorama]], although this was hardly enforceable, and was often ignored by the [[Abanderats|''abanderats'']].

Xacano [[W:Conquistadors|conquistadors]] extensively explored the coastal region of what is now Bayara, establishing extensive claims motivated by their desire to exploit of the abundant brasilwood and other natural resources, versus the Volisanian endeavors which were facilitating the forced resettlement of persecuted ethno-religious groups (such as [[Catharism|Cathars]], [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], [[Mozarabia|Mozarabs]] and {{W|Mudejar}}s) in an effort to establish coastal footholds and trading posts and ultimately compete with the other Mesogean powers. The colony of [[Macaio]] was established on 11 January 1549, and was quickly followed by [[San Xoán de Baxes]], and [[Palmarés]] as they explored up the [[Tapajós River]]. 

In Spain, discontent amongst the the {{W|Moriscos}} under the jurisdiction of the {{W|Crown of Castile}} led to a rebellion 1568. The {{W|War of the Alpujarras}} (as it came to be called), was fought until 1571, when the Aragonese Crown intervened. By the rebellion’s end, a significant proportion of Castile's formerly Muslim population was expelled, dispersing across the [[W:Spanish Empire|Spanish Empire]], primarily across the Atlantic to Balisca. Sizable numbers also relocated from [[Marrascania]] in North Africa, out of fear of potential future persecution by the [[W:Council of Castile|Castilian council]].

However tensions remained high in Spain, and instead of expelling the Moriscos or compelling them to abandon the last vestiges of their Arabic culture, the ''Cortes'' proposed a resettlement program: any Mozarab household that relinquished its lands in Spain would receive lands of three times the acreage in Balisca (primarily in the territory of Bayara) or a stately home and a 5-year annual stipend of 24,000 reales in any Baliscan land under Aragonese patronage. The sudden influx of [[Accelerationism|Ajami]]-speaking Christians and Muslims also led to small scale conversions among the native population that remained amongst them.

Relative to other Illypnian colonies, particularly those of the Spanish, the footholds on the Bayaran coast were considered exceptionally "poor and dangerous" place. In 1609, King [[W:Philip III of Spain|Philip III of Spain]] decreed the [[W:Expulsion of the Moriscos|Expulsion of the Moriscos]], resulting in the dispersal of over three million Moriscos across the the Spanish Empire. The Castilian council forcibly moved many Moriscos to Balisca in an attempt bolster their claims to the archipelago.
[[File:Théodore Valerio, Pâtre valaque de Zabalcz, Romanian shepherd from Zăbalț, 1852.jpg|thumb|293x293px|''El Rodamón'' ("The Vagabond") painting, by [[W:Théodore Valerio|Théodore Valerio]]. The ''rodamón'' is considered the national personification of Cerdanya.]]
Volisan explorers, under the leadership of navigators like [[Álvaro de Almagro]], pioneered the exploration of the island of Tinguiririca (paying special attention to the [[Strait of Ivorra]]) and established the first tentative settlements on it's southern shore. They brought with them Illypnian goods such as metal tools, textiles, and livestock, which they traded with the indigenous peoples in exchange for food, furs, and local knowledge. The initial encounters between the Aragonese and the indigenous populations were characterized by mutual curiosity and tentative trade.

Tensions between the Cathars and the Illypnian Catholics would eventually escalate into the [[Reapers' War]], during which the Spaniards destroyed several of the Cathar reductions and took many of the indigenous residents as slaves. In the aftermath of the conflict there was significant migration of [[W:Aranese dialect|Beron]] and [[W:Catalan language|Volisanian]]-speaking Cathars, fleeing religious persecution and expulsion from the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]] (then a part of colonial [[Bayara]]). These Cathars settled predominately on Tinguiririca's west coast, founding the [[Cerdanya|Colony of Cerdanya]]. ''Rodamóns'' (literally "wanderer" or "vagabond") is a term that was utilized in the 16th century to refer to the Cathars that were subjected to massacres and forced expulsions, first from [[Mesogean Sea|the Mesogean]], and then again from the Estuarian Wetlands by Catholics during the violent early colonization period. It was applied with a negative classist connotation to these poor refugees who were forced to flee southwards.

The Cathars, seeking to rebuild their society, brought with them Illypnian agricultural techniques, livestock, and crops. They established settlements that quickly grew into prosperous communities, such as [[Merced]], [[Rosario]] and [[Concepción]]. However, their arrival also spread old World diseases to which the indigenous populations had no immunity. Epidemics of [[W:Smallpox|smallpox]], [[W:Measles|measles]], and [[W:Influenza|influenza]] swept through Wallche and Yamana communities, causing catastrophic population declines. Entire villages were decimated, and the social fabric of indigenous societies was severely disrupted. The Cathars, despite their initial goodwill towards the indigenous peoples, found themselves in a position of dominance. The demographic collapse of the indigenous populations allowed the Cathars to expand their territories and influence more rapidly than they might have otherwise.

The Wallche and Yamana, though greatly diminished, continued to influence the evolving Cerdanyan society. Wallche agricultural techniques and crops were integrated into settler farming practices, and Yamana maritime skills proved invaluable in navigating and exploring the southern fjords and providing. The resulting syncretic culture was unique, blending Catharist, [[Volisan]], and indigenous elements into a distinct proto-Cerdanyan identity.

=== Slave revolts ===
{{Main|Maroon Wars|Maroon Confederacy}}

{{See also|Maroon pirates}}In 1521 Illypnian colonial authorities, relying heavily on [[Ethiopian slave trade|Kaftian slave traders]], passed a series of ordinances to limit the number of [[W:Islam in Africa|Muslim Kaftians]] taken to their colonies in the Algarbes. Many of these Kaftians were captives of war with military experience, and they were viewed as particularly dangerous. The colonial desire for slave labor outweighed their fears, which were well-founded as enslaved Kaftians (Muslims and non-Muslims alike) continued to rebel. Over the next three centuries there would continue to be violent instances of Kaftian revolts in the colonies of [[Bayara]], preventing the expansion of colonial settlements further into the interior from the coast.
[[File:Rébellion d'un esclave sur un navire négrier. E. Renard, 1839. MNM La Rochelle.JPG|thumb|[[Ayotunji]], an enslaved [[W:Yoruba people|Yoruba]] man turned pirate, whom led a successful revolt aboard the [[W:Schooner|schooner]] [[La Almunia|''La Almunia'']]]]
[[Daramazán]] was founded as a colony 1617 by escaped Mozarabic convicts and Kaftian slaves, at a time when the interior of the Northern Triangle, known as [[Maroon Confederacy|''Satanazes'']] ({{Literal translation|isle of devils}}) remained largely unexplored by Illypnians. As a refuge for those fleeing the colonial authorities, the territory became notorious for its gaudy displays of wealth and loose morals. It was a popular base for Maroon pirates from which to launch their raids on Iberian slave ships and settlements to liberate enslaved people, most of whom then joined the colony. The outbreak of slave revolts against the Xacano was to an extent supported by the colony, which helped to smuggle individuals to [[W:Quilombos|quilombos]]. This city later joined with the other quilombos to form the [[Maroon Confederacy]].

Between the outbreak of the first slave revolts in Bayara, and the [[Baliscan War of Independence|Volisan attempt to reconquer]] the territory in 1808, more than 375,000 formerly enslaved and free-born black people who faced social and legal oppression in the Baliscan colonies, along with 3,198 [[Kafto-Talokans]] were freed. Gradually developing an {{W|Creole}} identity heavily influenced by Indigenous, [[Kaftian people|Kaftian]] and [[Moro people|Moro]] groups, the settlers carried their culture and tradition with them.

The Maroon wars became the second and last slave uprising that led to the founding of a state which was both free from {{W|Slavery in the Americas|slavery}} and ruled by non-whites and former captives. It is now widely seen as a defining moment in the history of the [[Ethiopic world]]. The inhabitants developed a society and government that derived from a range of [[Indigenous peoples of the Algarbes|Indigenous]], [[Central Kaftia|Central]]-[[Western Kaftia|Western Kaftian]] sociopolitical models, with aspects of Illypnian culture and specifically local adaptations. This government was decentralized in nature, operating as a {{W|confederation}} of nominally independent towns and settlements united by their common goal of defending against threats posed by colonists. Eight Volisan expeditions invaded the territory in an attempt to conquer it between 1799 and 1804, but failed.

The revolution's effects on the institution of slavery were felt throughout the Algarbes. The disunity of the {{W|Peninsular War|besieged}} colonial governments, and the ''de facto'' abolition of slavery in the colony was followed by a successful defense of the freedoms the former slaves won and, with the collaboration of already previously free people of color, solidified their independence from Illypnians. The revolt in Balisca represented the largest slave uprising in the history of the Western Hemisphere and challenged long-held Illypnian beliefs about slaves' ability to achieve and maintain their own freedom. The rebels' organizational capacity and tenacity under pressure ultimately contributed to the 1809 [[Proclamation of Alcabaza]] which ''{{W|de jure}}'' ended the practice of slavery in Balisca.

=== Winds of revolution and ''Resistencía'' ===
{{Main|Volisanian Civil War|Volisanya Boba|}}

{{See also|Commonwealth of Cerdanya|United Provinces of Bayara|Hesperian League}}

The [[Volisanya Boba]] period (1736–1808) coincided with the [[Volisanian Civil War]] and the subsequent devolution of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]].   Following the [[secession of Reige]] in 1736, the United Kingdoms entered a prolonged constitutional crisis that would consume Volisania for the next 65  years. The outbreak of civil war immediately disrupted colonial administration, as colonial governors received contradictory instructions from competing metropolitan factions and Volisanian naval protection for colonial shipping declined significantly. By the 1750s, it had become clear that Volisania would be unable to reassert effective control for the foreseeable future, and the Baliscan concessions entered a period of de facto autonomy.  
[[File:Revolt_of_the_Comuneros.jpg|left|thumb|The [[Revolt of the Comuneros|1738 peasant revolt]] in the city of [[Merced]]. The painting depicts the ''comuneros'' taking up the image of the {{W|Holy Christ}} as the banner of the uprising, in [[Rodrigo de la Sierra]]'s 1908 portrait, ''[[Revolta dels Comuneros (painting)|Revolta dels Comuneros]]'']]
The breakdown of Volisanian authority during the civil war created conditions that profoundly threatened the traditional autonomy of the Baliscan concessions. While Volisania did not implement decrees equivalent to the  Sartan consolidation policies, the civil war created similar pressures toward centralization when metropolitan factions attempted to revoke concessional privileges and integrate them directly into the metropole's  administration. The colonial population, at this time composed mainly of Cathars, Sephardic Jews, Muslims, Ajami, and Bayani converts, staunchly opposed these centralization efforts. Having experienced persecution in their  various homelands, they adamantly rejected any diminution of the autonomy they had enjoyed under the concession system. Their resistance was fueled by a desire to preserve their cultural identities and religious practices.  

Simmering unrest throughout the period led to the [[Merced Uprising]] of 1738 in Cerdanya, where colonial communities established the [[Junta of Merced]] to coordinate resistance against metropolitan interference. The uprising, depicted in [[Rodrigo de la Sierra]]'s 1908 portrait ''Revolta dels Komuneros'', shows the colonists taking up the image of the Holy Christ as the  banner of their resistance. The Junta would eventually evolve into the [[Commonwealth of Cerdanya]], a self-governing entity that operated independently throughout the remainder of the civil war period. Similar developments occurred in Hesperia, where the [[Hesperian League]] expanded its influence, and in the western territories, where the [[United Provinces of Bayara|United Provinces of the Marañón]] emerged as a centralized political entity. 

The period also saw significant expansion of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] and the proliferation of [[Maroon pirates|Maroon corsair]] operations throughout the Mesogean Sea.  Without effective Volisanian naval protection, Maroon communities expanded inland and developed sophisticated maritime capabilities. Their initial focus  on disrupting the slave trade expanded to target Volisanian shipping generally, as Maroons aligned themselves with the broader resistance to  imperial authority. The Maroon corsair campaigns became particularly effective during the 1770s, undermining Volisania's logistical efforts and  further aligning the Maroon Confederacy with the anti-centralization cause.  

The conclusion of the Volisanian Civil War with the [[Law of the Community of Volisania Acts 1801 and 1812]] fundamentally changed the relationship between  Volisania and its colonial possessions. The newly constituted Community of Volisania, seeking to reassert control and recover revenues lost during the civil war, launched an [[Baliscan War of Independence|attempt to reassert imperial authority]] in 1808. In response, 18 colonial communities issued the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]] in 1809, uniting as the [[Northern Confederation]] to resist the Volisanian attempt at reconquest.
=== ''Volisanyado'' and ''Resistencía'' ===
{{Main|Volisanyado|Continuation War}}

In 1801, the conclusion of the [[Volisanian Civil War]] with the [[Law of the Community of Volisania Acts 1801 and 1812]] brought a new political faction to power within the newly constituted Community of Volisania. The [[Restaurasionistas|Restaurasionistas faction]], led by Prime Minister [[Enric de Montclar]], sought to reverse the decades of colonial autonomy that had characterized the Volisanya Boba period and reintegrate all colonial possessions directly into the Commonwealth structure. Montclar's government, dominated by metropolitan interests who had suffered immensely economically during the civil war, viewed the recovery of colonial revenues as essential to the Commonwealth's financial stability.

The ambitions of the Volisanian Centralists found willing collaborators among the Bayaran economic elite, with many plantation owners aligning with the metropolitan government in the hopes of preserving the institution of slavery upon which their profitable plantation economy was based. Slavery was under increasing pressure in light of growing abolitionist sentiment among Hesperian and Cerdanyan republicans. The ideological rift intensified in reaction to the [[Granyana Convention]] of 1806, in which the emergent [[Northern Confederation]] pledged to "fully eradicate" the practice and redistribute land to freedmen. The prospect of losing both property and political dominance galvanized many Bayaran landowners, who viewed Volisanian intervention as a means to restore a social and economic order increasingly under threat. However, beyond this alliance of convenience, the Bayarans had little affection for metropolitan authority.  [[File:Battle_of_Navassa.png|thumb|Defeat of the Royalist garrison at the  [[Battle of Navassa]]]]In September 1808, Bayara's legislature—the [[General Junta]]—dominated by plantation owners and metropolitan sympathizers, issued a proclamation of  allegiance to the "legitimate government of the Community of Volisania," citing the need for "stability, order, and the defense of property and civilization." Shortly thereafter, the Junta granted permission for Volisanian expeditionary forces—dispatched from metropolitan garrisons—to land at key Bayaran ports, including [[San Xoán de Baxes]] and [[Navassa]]. These landings marked the first foreign military incursion into Baliscan territory in nearly a century and represented the beginning of Volisania's attempt to reassert imperial authority. Hesperia and Cerdanya denounced the move as a betrayal of their longstanding political independence and their opposition to metropolitan centralization. In response to the invasion, eighteen colonial communities issued the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]] in 1809, uniting as the [[Northern Confederation]] and formally declaring resistance to Volisanian reconquest—officially launching the [[Baliscan War of Independence]].  

In November 1808, the first major naval clash occurred off the coast of [[Els Farallones]], a rocky channel between the [[Algarves]] and the coast of [[Aprica]]. A convoy of Volisanian-flagged supply ships, escorted by a lightly armed brigantine, attempted to breach the blockade to deliver weapons and reinforcements to [[Macaio]]. They were intercepted by a flotilla of [[Maroon corsairs]] operating in coordination with the Northern Confederation. The  resulting battle was brief: three vessels were captured, one was scuttled, and several prisoners confessed to carrying sealed orders from [[Ampuria]] (the Volisan capital), revealing the full scale of the imperial reconquest plan. In 1815, Volisanian forces were decisively defeated in the [[Battle of Navassa]], effectively destroying the last metropolitan strongholds in mainland Balisca and thus allowing the confederalists to consolidate control and end the threat of reconquest against the Confederation. While this did not immediately change Volisania's official position against Baliscan separatism, it resulted in the collapse of metropolitan forces in Balisca. Over the course of the next two years, the anti-metropolitan armies won major victories and consolidated their control in their respective provinces. The continued political instability in Volisania, without the resources to maintain a distant colonial war, convinced many former metropolitan sympathizers of the need to formally abandon their cause and embrace independence. The [[Treaty of Bixessarri]] of 1838 would eventually formalize the independence of the Baliscan Confederation, bringing the long process that began with the end of Volisanya Boba to its conclusion.
=== Early confederation and civil war ===
{{Main|Confederal Question|Compromise of 1827|Baliscan Civil War}}

{{See also|Maroon resettlement}}
[[File:Zouave_-_Grolleron_crop.jpg|left|thumb|298x298px|Cerdanyan ''[[Zuau]]'' in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], {{circa|1825}}]]
Immediately following the [[Treaty of Navassa|securing of independence]], dissatisfaction with the confederal system of governance emerged. The republics began to look to their own interests rather than the confederation. During the war, confederal forces had imprisoned, hanged, or expelled all former slave owners (most relocated to [[Krauseland]]), and divided their land and other assets between the newly emancipated Kaftian population. This further hardened attitudes in Bayara against confederation, which already was strong due to the devastation in Bayara in terms of infrastructure and economy. Because of the reluctance of the land-owning class accept the loss of their privileges and to grant voting rights to freedmen, the Confederal Congress adopted [[Reconstruction laws]] over Bayaran objections. In response, and intending to avoid the social effects of the war, most of the Bayaran provinces passed [[W:Black codes|black codes]]. These were mostly ''de jure'' nullified by confederal law and pro-reformist legislatures, but widely continued to be enforced by Bayaran officials. Increasing calls for the strengthening of confederal power and increasing confederal influence in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]] fueled inter-communal tensions between predominately Volisanian-speaking [[Catharism|Cathars]] and Xacano-speaking Catholics, and the confederal government was increasingly dragged into the position of mediator in the region. Conflict boiled over following the murder of an Volisanian [[W:Pastry|pastry]] chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob, resulting in [[Pastry War|escalating reprisals and counter-reprisals]] which eventually culminated in [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]], which was ultimately ended by the [[Treaty of Navassa]].

The treaty led to the ''de facto'' partition of Bayara on [[W:Ethnoreligious group|ethnoreligious]] lines, which was propped up by the intervention of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] (popularly known as the ''zuau'') to administer the Estuarian territories, which largely prevented further violence. Bayaran dissatisfaction with the terms of the Treaty led to it's delegates blocking the allocation of confederal funding for the Legion in the [[Confederal Congress]], ultimately forcing their withdrawal in the spring of 1826.

Hesperian delegates called for a [[Confederal Convention]] to solve the impending crisis, to be held in [[Alessandria]]. The convention resulted in the [[Compromise of 1827]] which provided for a significantly strengthened central government to handle disputes between the respective republics and their citizens. This was unanimously supported by the delegations of Hesperia and [[Cerdanya]], but was rejected and ultimately ignored by the Bayarans, with the exception of the delegation from [[Macaio]]. These delegates were at the time aware that the republic was well on it's way towards renouncing the [[Articles of Confederation]] and {{W|secession|seceding}} from the Confederation, and sought to pre-empt this by declaring the [[Carpinchera Republic]]. The General Junta denounced the Articles of Confederation on 6 July 1828, and the secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]] assumed provisional powers in anticipation of war.
[[File:Pedro Américo - Batalha do Avaí.jpg|thumb|330x330px|''Batalla de Oristany'' by [[Pedro Américo]], depicting the first battle of the Baliscan Civil War]]
Despite controlling a numerical troop advantage throughout the war, the Bayarans were forced to divert significant numbers of troops towards their efforts to subdue the intensifying uprisings and ambushes by Maroons and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] resisting the Xunta's policy of [[Maroon resettlement|forced removal]] from the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Secessionist forces would go on to wage of violent campaign of expulsion, in the process successfully destroying dozens of ''[[W:Quilombos|quilombos]]'' and expelling their inhabitants to the territory of the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Notably however, Bayaran armies crossed the [[Cerroitá Pass]] and attempted to capture the [[Alpujarra]], culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]], which was defeated with the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] in support of pro-confederation forces.

By 1835, most Bayaran pro-secessionist land and naval forces had either disbanded, surrendered or otherwise ceased hostilities. Despite this the war lacked a formal end, with bands of secessionist guerrilla forces fighting, surrendering or disbanding sporadically throughout most of 1835-1837. Tensions reignited after a massacre in [[Brumavent]], in which pro-secessionist soldiers executed the hundreds of captives and vowed to continue their guerrilla warfare. This triggered significant unrest on both sides and reignited the already high tensions between the Catalans and Aragonese that resided in the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Reprisals and counter reprisals escalated, and the two factions subsequently fought a series of battles across the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], from which the pro-confederation forces again emerged victorious.

Bayara suffered substantially more than the rest of the confederation overall, as the confederalist strategy of [[W:Attrition warfare|attrition warfare]] meant that Bayaran forces could not replace their casualties, and the total war waged by Hesperian armies devastated the infrastructure and caused widespread poverty and distress. The seceding provinces were punished severely in the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]]: they were forced to pay the expenses and debts incurred by the Confederation during the war, in addition to the the Legislative Assembly's being coerced into ratifying the Compromise, resulting what became known as the [[Ani Federali|Reconstruction era]]. Communes and regions within Bayara that had sided with the confederalists following the [[Brumavent massacre]] largely avoided occupation. In addition, the Confederal Congress recognized the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]], establishing {{W|amical protection|amical relations}} with [[Maroon Confederacy|Maroon Territory]].

=== Federalization and industrialization ===
{{Main|Anyos Federales|Cosmocazo}}

The term Anyos Federales ({{literally|federal years}}), also known as "Reconstruction"''',''' refers to a period of [[W:Nation-building|nation-building]] in Baliscan history following the conclusion of it's [[Baliscan Civil War|extended civil war]], which resulted in the country's federalization under it's first president, [[Michelangelo Valentino]]. The economy prospered during the later years of the civil war, in part due to the fielding of a large confederal army and navy which heavily relied on raw materials from Cerdanya. This led to a diffuse, if mostly artisanal, concentration of manufacturing activities, especially under the economically liberal policies adopted by the emerging federal administration in the aftermath of the civil war. The [[Industrial Revolution in Balisca|Baliscan Industrial Revolution]] was launched along the [[Strait of Ivorra]] in 1840, where [[W:Textile mills|textile mills]] spread across [[Merced]], and in southern [[Cadaqués]], where coal, steel, and industrialization launched the republic's first experiments in the manufacturing sector. Government sponsored-programs enacted by the empowered [[Confederal Congress]] during this time encouraged an unprecedented wave of [[Immigration to Balisca|immigrants to Balisca]], resulting in the introduction of influxes of cheap labor and the influence of Illypnian and Fosian capitalist intervention in Baliscan economic and social policy. Early investments in industry and the development of the railway network during the 1840s came almost exclusively from private hands. Without a central labor regulatory agencies, these construction projects were quickly realized, often at great loss of life. Actual industrialization only took off after 1850 in the wake of the railroad construction, and for the next two decades Baliscan industry grew exponentially. 
[[File:Ángel DELLA Valle - La vuelta del malón - Google Art Project (cropped).jpg|thumb|''La vuelta del malón'' by [[W:Ángel della Valle|Ángel della Valle]] (<abbr>c.</abbr> 1894), depicting a [[W:Malón|malón]] during the [[Tinguiririca conflict]]]]
In 1853 gold was discovered in southern Cerdanya (present day [[Muntanya]] and [[Provence]]), sparking the [[Cerdanyan gold rush]]. The allure of potentially striking gold and achieving wealth brought approximately 300,000 people to Cerdanya, both from the rest of the confederation and from abroad, many of them [[Illypnian Baliscans|Illypnian]], [[Arab Baliscans|Bayani]], [[Jade Baliscans|Jade]] and smaller numbers of [[Kaftia#South Kaftia|South Kaftians]]. The influx of gold into the money supply reinvigorated the sluggish Baliscan economy; and the rapid population increase allowed for Cerdanya to rapidly accumulate [[Volisan aristocracy|outsized economic and political power]] in the confederation. However, the gold rush had severe effects on the Indigenous people of Tinguiririca, accelerating their population's decline from disease, starvation, intensifying the [[Tinguiririca conflict]] with increased settler-colonial encroachment on their territories.
[[File:EstacionCentralBuenosAires.png|thumb|Several trains stopped at [[Central Station of Macaio]], terminal of the [[Carpinchera and Quiscosta Railway]].  Former [[Macaio Customs Building]] with its jetty in the background (1874)|left]]
With the birth of the [[Anyos Federales|federal state]], there was a deep consciousness in the [[Cataloccitan aristocracy|emerging ruling class]] of the country's slow development, given that the per capita GDP expressed in PPS terms was roughly half of that of Breisland and about 25% less than that of [[Hersatia]] and [[Burawa]]. During the 1860s and 1870s, the manufacturing activity was relatively artisan and small-scale, while the oversized agrarian sector was the backbone of the confederation economy. The country however had large [[W:Coal|coal]] and iron deposits, which allowed a rapid expansion in [[W:Steam power|steam power]] and advances in iron and [[W:Steel production|steelmaking]]. Conflicts with Indigenous peoples regarding territorial exploration and settlement were arbitrated by the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. This prompted the introduction of more modern farming techniques in the rural interior, while from 1878 to 1887 protectionist policies were introduced with the aim to establish a heavy industry base. Large steel and iron works soon clustered around areas of high hydropower potential, notably the foothills of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], and Merced and Concepción captured the textile, chemicals, engineering and banking boom.

[[W:Social inequality|Social inequality]] and [[W:Corruption|corruption]] emerged as concerning issues as time went on, and the provinces faced criticism for their handling of [[W:Social issues|social issues]]. The millions of people migrating to the Baliscan cities quickly led to overcrowding, sanitation issues and crime. The influx of people put immense pressure on the existing transport and sanitation networks, which were not equipped to handle such a large number of people. As a result, many migrants were forced to live in unsanitary conditions, leading to outbreaks of diseases such as {{W|cholera}} and {{W|typhus}}. The rapidly growing immigrant population was often economically and socially marginalized and locked into relatively dangerous elicit means of survival until the emergence of railroads, [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade unions]], and universal education which strengthened the integration of rural Balisca in the late-1800s. In response to these social issues, progressive movements (including the faith-based [[Just Society Association|Associación Sociedad Justa]], the [[Jade Benevolent Association]], and the [[Christian Socialist Party]]) emerged, advocating for reforms to improve the living and working conditions of the urban poor.

These progressive movements worked to pressure legislators to pass labor laws that established [[W:Minimum wage|minimum wages]] (nationwide in 1906) and maximum work hours (nationwide in 1907), as well as [[Workplace Safety Act (1907)|safety regulations]] that aimed to prevent accidents and injuries in the workplace. They also pushed for the development of public transportation systems such as {{W|trams}} (allowing for the expansion of [[W:Streetcar suburb|streetcar suburbs]]) and the eventual development of {{W|rapid transit}}, along with the construction of adequate sanitation networks, aiming to improve conditions and reduce the spread of disease. The [[Castejón Metro]] (then called the "Subte"), opened in 1909, becoming the first rapid transit network in Abyala, followed by the [[Concepción Underground]] and [[Alessandria Metro]] in 1914 and 1915. 
==== Gilded Age and increasing radicalization ====
{{Main|Baliscan Gilded Age|Cantonal Revolution}}
[[File:Summit_of_Nations.png|left|thumb|300x300px|''La Cimeira de les Naciones'' (the "Summit of Nations") by [[Xabier Sánchez]] depicts the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] and drafting of the ''[[Cosmocazo]]''-era constitution.]]
In the later years of the ''Anyos Federales'', the establishment was threatened by the rise of the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' movement, which arose from those opposed to the displays of extravagance and excesses of President [[Michelangelo Valentino]], his cabinet and the wealthy elites, the slow pace of political and social reforms, and the [[W:centralism (Peru)|increasing centralization]] of political and economic power in [[Castejón]]. Later, the idea of {{W|cosmopolitanism}} entered mainstream Baliscan society. ''La Cosmópolis'' by [[Marc Soler]] greatly influenced national thought, furthering the idea of this new state being a union of numerous ethnic groups and calling for historical reconciliation and opposition to sectarianism. This was enshrined in the Constitution of 1883, which defined Balisca as the first "cosmopolitan state". The tenure Valentino's successor, [[Josèp Bernat]] was fraught with growing radicalism, as an increasingly authoritative Bernat utilized aggressive measures to crush growing dissent with violent force by [[Regions of Balisca|provincial militias]]. After three tumultuous years in office, Bernat (operating from [[Maracaju]]) resigned on 8 June 1876, and fled to [[Krauseland]]. The [[1883 Confederal Convention]] would overwhelmingly consist of the [[W:Cantonalism|Cantonalists]], and the confederation was subsequently reorganized into [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]] and [[Communes of Balisca|communes]], while provincial powers were abolished. {{W|Secular humanism}} and {{W|anti-clericalism}} witnessed surges in popularity during this time.  [[File:Flag of Partido Liberal Mexicano.svg|thumb|Flag of the [[W:Cantonalism|Cantonalists]], and a symbol of the 1883 Confederal Convention]]

Within the Constitution's articles, [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous sovereignty]] was recognized and protected, environmental conservation prohibited {{W|religious schools}} in prohibited political parties, and granted sweeping land reforms. The proposed Cantonalist Constitution called for policies that tended toward a [[W:Horizontalidad|horizontal]] and anti-religious system of [[W:Soviet democracy|council democracy]], which included the {{W|separation of church and state|total separation of religion from public life}}, [[W:Self-governance|self-policing]], the abolition of rent, the abolition of {{W|child labor}}, and the universal [[W:workers' self-management|right of employees to take over an enterprise]]. Active (predominately Catholic) churches and religious schools declined substantially due to their prior support for the presidency of Bernat, in contrast with most communities which were more receptive to the cantonal cause. {{W|Feminist}}, {{W|socialist}}, [[W:Communism|communist]] and [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] currents played important roles in the shaping of this new system. 

===== Economic transformation and inequality =====
{{Further|Centralism}}[[File:Bird's-eye views of Woolworth Bldg. under construction, New York City - June 22, 1912 LCCN2002705857.jpg|left|thumb|The [[Empúries Bank Building]] under-construction in [[Ampurias]], [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]] in 1912. It was one of the first Baliscan "[[W:Early skyscrapers|early skyscrapers]]".]]
The decades following the Cantonal Revolution witnessed unprecedented economic transformation in Balisca, with exploitation of the country's abundant natural resources—including coal, iron, copper, and agricultural land—attracting substantial foreign investment, particularly from [[Volisania]], [[Mercia]], and [[Burawa]]. The expansion of the railway network accelerated dramatically, with track mileage increasing from 2,300 kilometers in 1883 to over 18,000 kilometers by 1910.

This economic growth, however, was distributed unevenly, and the benefits of industrialization were concentrated in the hands of a small elite composed of the old [[Volisan aristocracy]], newly wealthy industrialists, and foreign investors. Real wages for industrial workers remained stagnant between 1885 and 1910, while the cost of living in urban areas increased by approximately 60 percent. In the agricultural sector, the consolidation of land ownership accelerated as small farmers were displaced by large estates, creating a growing class of landless rural laborers.

The [[W:Income inequality|gap between rich and poor]] was most apparent in Baliscan urban areas. In [[Castejón]], [[Alessandria]], and [[Macaio]], wealthy industrialists and aristocrats were pioneering the [[W:Early skyscrapers|first Baliscan skyscrapers]] and constructing lavish mansions along tree-lined boulevards, while workers crowded into [[W:Tenements|tenements]] lacking adequate sanitation, clean water, or ventilation. Public health statistics from the period reveal the consequences of this inequality: infant mortality rates in working-class neighborhoods were three to four times higher than in wealthy districts, and outbreaks of [[W:Cholera|cholera]], [[W:Typhus|typhus]], and [[W:Tuberculosis|tuberculosis]] were regular occurrences in the poorest areas.

===== Political manipulation and machine politics =====
The political reforms granted in the aftermath of the Cantonal Revolution proved to be far more limited in practice than they appeared on paper. [[W:Universal male suffrage|Universal male suffrage]], implemented with great fanfare in 1883, was implemented in ways that significantly restricted its practical effect: [[W:Literacy test|Literacy requirements]] effectively disenfranchised the substantial portion of the population that could not read or write in [[Baliscano]], disproportionately affecting indigenous communities and recent immigrants. Residency requirements mandated that voters must have lived in their electoral district for at least two years before casting a ballot, excluding the large numbers of mobile workers who moved frequently in search of employment. The combination of these restrictions meant that while the nominal electorate had expanded dramatically, actual voter turnout remained low and the political influence of the working class remained limited.

The [[W:Right to unionize|right to unionize]], recognized in 1884, was similarly constrained by qualifications that blunted its impact. The legislation permitted only [[W:Craft unions|craft unions]] that organized workers by their specific trade, explicitly prohibiting [[W:Industrial unions|industrial unions]] that would unite all workers in a particular industry regardless of skill level. This restriction prevented the development of the broad-based labor organizations that had proven most effective in other countries, fragmenting the working class along craft lines and limiting the bargaining power of individual unions. Furthermore, unions were prohibited from engaging in political activity, endorsing candidates, or participating in electoral campaigns, depriving workers of the ability to translate their economic organizing into political influence. The right to strike was severely restricted by mandatory arbitration procedures that required workers to submit disputes to government-appointed mediators before any work stoppage could legally occur. The arbitration process was lengthy, typically lasting months, during which workers were expected to continue working under the conditions they were protesting. Employers frequently used the arbitration period to [[W:Strikebreaker|hire replacement workers]], stockpile inventory, or prepare for a strike, while workers exhausted their savings waiting for permission to act. When strikes did occur without government approval, they were declared illegal and participants were subject to arrest and dismissal.

Many of the democratic institutions and reforms that were conceded by the central government proved to be hollow and vulnerable to manipulation by wealthy interests. The [[Confederalist Party]] (PC), founded in 1887 by a coalition of aristocratic families, industrialists, and conservative politicians, developed a sophisticated political machine that came to dominate Baliscan politics by the turn of the century. The PC was effectively an alliance at the federal level between the parties that dominated their respective Republics: the [[Bayaran Republican Party]], the [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)]] and the . Their political strategy relied on several mechanisms: 

* '''Patronage networks''': The party distributed government positions, contracts, and favors to build networks of dependent supporters. At its peak, the Confederalists employed an estimated 50,000 patronage workers across Balisca, creating a class of politically connected individuals whose livelihoods depended on the party's continued dominance.
* '''Electoral manipulation''': Although the 1883 Constitution guaranteed universal male suffrage, the PC developed techniques for controlling electoral outcomes. These included manipulation of voter registration lists, intimidation of opposition supporters, ballot stuffing, and the strategic deployment of local officials sympathetic to the party. Elections during this period were routinely contested, with opposition candidates frequently denouncing results as fraudulent.

* '''Media control''': The Confederalists and their allies acquired controlling interests in most major newspapers, ensuring favorable coverage of the party and its candidates. Independent journalists faced harassment, legal action, and occasionally violence when they attempted to report on corruption or criticize the political establishment.

* '''Clientelism''': In working-class neighborhoods and rural areas, Confederalist operatives distributed food, money, and other benefits to voters in exchange for electoral support. This system created dependency relationships that undermined the development of independent political consciousness among the poor. Voters who relied on party patronage for their survival could not afford to support opposition candidates, regardless of their personal political beliefs. The result was a political system that appeared democratic on the surface but was fundamentally controlled by economic elites who had learned to manipulate the mechanisms of representative government to their advantage.

The effectiveness of these techniques was demonstrated in the [[1895 Baliscan confederal election]], in which the Confederalist Party and it's allies won 67 percent of seats in the [[Confederal Congress]] despite receiving only an estimated 35 percent of the popular vote. Opposition parties, fragmented and under-resourced, proved unable to mount an effective challenge.

===== Constitutional subversion =====
{{further|1905 Baliscan constitutional amendments}}
The most significant threat to the post-war constitutional order came through formal amendments that gradually stripped away the limited powers that had been granted to cantons and workers. Beginning in 1903, the Confederal Congress passed a series of constitutional reforms that fundamentally altered the balance of power in Balisca, reversing what few concessions had been made in the aftermath of the Cantonal Revolution. The reforms of 1905 were particularly consequential and represented a systematic effort to restore the centralization that had existed before the rebellions. They eliminated cantonal authority over labor regulation, education, and policing, returning these powers to the federal government. The reforms also granted the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal]] [[Confederal government of Balisca|government]] the authority to dismiss cantonal governments and replace them with appointed administrators, a power that was used extensively to remove officials suspected of sympathy for labor or left-wing causes.
[[File:Por dónde viene la muerte, de Demócrito.jpg|left|thumb|320x320px|''Where death comes from'' (1905) depicting prominent capitalists of the time dismantling democracy in Balisca.]]
Additionally, they restored [[W:Property qualification|property requirements for voting]], effectively reversing the expansion of suffrage that had been granted in 1883. The new requirements mandated that voters must own property valued at a level that excluded approximately 60 percent of adult male citizens. The stated justification for this change was to ensure that voters had a "stake in society" and would exercise the franchise responsibly, but critics correctly identified it as an attempt to exclude the working class from political participation. The restoration of property qualifications represented an explicit admission that the post-war reforms had granted too much [[W:Political power|political power]] to those who might use it to challenge elite interests.

Additional reforms in 1908 and 1910 further concentrated power in confederal institutions. "[[W:sedition|Anti-sedition]]" legislation authorized the dissolution of unions and political organizations deemed threatening to public order, with the determination of what constituted a threat left to federal officials whose appointments were controlled by the wealthy interests. Changes to the judicial appointment process ensured that courts would be staffed by judges sympathetic to elite interests, reversing earlier reforms that had given cantons a role in selecting local judges. By 1910, the constitutional framework that had been established in the aftermath of the Cantonal Revolution had been largely dismantled, and the political system had returned to a level of centralization and exclusion that rivaled the Valentino era.

===== Labor organizing and militant resistance =====
The erosion of cantonal autonomy and the consolidation of elite power provoked a resurgence of labor organizing. The [[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]] (FAT) founded in 1870 as a federation of craft unions and operated clandestinely until 1885, grew dramatically during this period, expanding from 15,000 members in 1890 to over 400,000 by 1915. It was followed by the [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]] (CAT), founded in 1892, which had around 250,000 enrolled members by the summer of 1917.
[[File:Baliscan sailors power station.webp|thumb|Sailors of the [[Confederal Navy]] taking over the operation of the [[Navassa Irrigation and Power Company]] during the 1915 general strike.]]
The labor movement in Balisca developed distinctive characteristics that distinguished it from counterparts in other countries in [[Abyala]], [[Illypnia]] and [[Fosia]], which were often divided along ethnic, national, or religious lines, the FAT-CAT coalition successfully organized workers across ethnic boundaries. Indigenous workers, immigrants from Illypno-Fosia and Kaftia, and native-born Baliscans all participated in the same unions and political organizations. Several factors contributed to this unity, including their dominant ideology, anarcho-syndicalism, which emphasized class solidarity over ethnic or national identity. The coalition's charter explicitly prohibited discrimination on the basis of ethnicity, national origin, or religion, building on tradition of cross-ethnic political cooperation that persisted following the failed [[Cantonal Revolution]].

The FAT/CAT coalition and its affiliates engaged in increasingly militant actions during the 1910s. The [[1915 Baliscan general Strike|1915 general strike]], which involved over 300,000 workers across Balisca, paralyzed the economy for three weeks before being suppressed by military force. The strike had been called to demand the restoration of cantonal autonomy, the repeal of [[W:Anti-sedition laws|anti-sedition laws]], and the recognition of industrial unions, demands that went far beyond the "bread-and-butter issues" that had previously dominated labor activism. The strike's defeat, and the arrest of many union leaders, radicalized significant portions of the labor movement, convincing many activists that fundamental change could not be achieved through legal means. However, the unions achieved some successes at the cantonal and communal levels, particularly in areas where the PC's machine was weaker. Several cantons enacted minimum wage laws, workplace safety regulations, and requirements for adequate housing. However, these local reforms were frequently overturned by confederal authorities following the 1905 constitutional changes.

====== Resurgent militarism and the road to revolution ======
{{Main|Red Summer|Anarchism in Balisca}}

The 1917 collapse of the [[Banc de Castejón]] and the subsequent [[1917 Baliscan banking crisis|banking/financial crisis]] triggered by speculative investments and outright fraud, wiped out the savings of hundreds of thousands of middle-class and working-class families. The bank's directors had used depositors' funds to finance risky ventures and personal enrichment, assuming that the bank's political connections would protect them from consequences. When the [[W:Speculation|speculation]] failed and the bank could not meet its obligations, the federal government intervened to protect prop-up large creditors and foreign investors while doing little for ordinary depositors. What occurred afterwards was an increase in [[W:Wealth concentration|concentration of wealth]], widespread [[W:Bankruptcy|bankruptcy]], as well as ignorance and distrust regarding how politics and markets work, in addition to the geometric increase of [[W:Debt|debt]] and economic stagnation. 

Following the effects of the [[W:Boom and bust|bust]], came a violent period of [[W:Witch hunt#Metaphorical usage|"witch hunts"]], marked by [[W:Propaganda of the deed|targeted attacks]] against those deemed responsible. The first major attack occurred on 18 May 1917, when a bomb exploded at the entrance to the [[Secretariat of Finance and Civil Service (Balisca)|Secretariat of Finance]] in Castejón, killing three guards and injuring fifteen others. A manifesto claiming responsibility, signed by a previously unknown group calling itself the ''[[Comité de Acción Revolucionaria]]'', denounced the government's handling of the banking crisis and declared that "those who steal from the people will answer to the people." Over the following weeks, similar attacks targeted regional finance offices, the homes of bank directors, and the headquarters of corporations implicated in the scandal. The violence escalated in June when a coordinated wave of [[W:Letter bomb|letter bombs]] was sent to prominent politicians, corporate executives, and newspaper editors who had defended the government's response to the crisis. At least twelve people were killed and dozens more injured in these attacks, which spread panic through the upper echelons of Baliscan society.

The most dramatic incident of the Red Summer was the assassination of [[Ramón Vilar]], a powerful [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|''Progressista'']] boss who had built the political machine that dominated [[Merced]] and who had personally profited from the banking scandal through his connections to the Banc de Castejón's directors. On 15 July 1917, Vilar was shot dead by a lone gunman while leaving a restaurant in [[La Rambla, Merced|La Rambla]], Merced. The assassin, identified as a young anarchist and former bank clerk named [[Eusebio Molins]], was arrested at the scene and reportedly declared that he had acted to "avenge the thousands of families ruined by the bank's collapse. Molins became a martyr figure in radical circles, and his trial in September 1917 was marked by massive protests and clashes between demonstrators and police. Molins was sentenced to [[W:Life in prison|life in prison]] as [[Capital punishment in Balisca|capital punishment had been abolished]] in 1873.

The anti-sedition laws passed in 1908 were invoked to arrest hundreds of suspected radicals, including many who had no connection to the violence but were simply prominent in labor or socialist organizations. Newspapers that reported sympathetically on the grievances underlying the attacks were shut down, and journalists were prosecuted for "inciting disorder." The repression created a feedback loop of radicalization, as moderate reformers were discredited and imprisoned while the most militant factions gained credibility and support.

By 1918, Balisca was a society on the brink of revolutionary upheaval. The labor movement was larger and more militant than ever before, with a leadership increasingly committed to fundamental change rather than incremental reform that could be easily rescinded. Progressive organizations had built networks of support across the country, creating channels for communication and coordination that had not existed during the Cantonal Revolution. Confidence in political institutions had collapsed, and even many "middle-class" Baliscans who had previously supported the existing order were increasing disillusioned with it, questioning whether it could be reformed or needed to be replaced. The election of [[Pol Pérez]] in October 1918 would prove to be the spark that ignited the [[Baliscan Revolution]], but the conditions for revolution had been building for decades.

=== Baliscan Revolution ===
{{Main|Baliscan Revolution|Processo Revolucionario en Curso}}

{{See also|Baliscan post-revolution exodus|Right-wing insurgency in Balisca}}
Prosperity stemming from the reforms adopted during the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] led to the development of [[Health care in Balisca|universal health care]] (1914), [[Baliscan Pension Plan|old-age pensions]] (1915), and [[Veterans Affairs (Balisca)|veterans' pensions]] (1915). In addition, universal free education allowed for sizable increases in educational enrollment and development, giving rise to a new generation of social development across the Confederation. The economy grew 12% annually, surpassing that of the rest of the [[ABC countries]] combined. Restrictions on imports forced by the [[W:First World War|First World War]] and government policies of "import substitution" and trade tariffs, all contributed to industrial growth. However, this golden age was not without its shadows. The rapid, [[W:Export-oriented industrialization|export-driven growth]] led to increasing [[W:Societal stratification|societal stratification]] and the [[W:Distribution of wealth#Wealth concentration|concentration of wealth]]. The [[Volisan aristocracy]] in Cerdanya wielded disproportionate economic and political power at the confederal level, causing simmering unrest amongst the other republics. As Baliscan wages grew much higher than those in the [[W:Periphery countries|periphery countries]], especially for skilled workers, and [[W:Industrialization|industrialization]] demanded an increasingly skilled labor force, the period saw an influx of millions of immigrants. The rapid expansion of industrialization led to real wage growth of 30% from 1880 to 1914 and spread across the increasing labor force.
[[File:Bandera FAT-CAT.svg|thumb|Joint flag of the [[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]] and the [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]].]]
The boom in immigration provided a market for goods, and sectors such as food processing grew. However the rapidly growing urban population grew increasingly resentful of the economic elite, which had combined its economic power with the electoral strength of immigrant communities and the employing of [[W:Machine politics|machine politics]] to maintain their dominant position. The stand-off was also political: politics in Bayara had been long monopolized by the [[Bayaran Republican Party]] (PRB), but by the eve of the Baliscan Revolution, left-wing ([[Anarchism in Balisca|predominately anarchist]]) movements had been growing in opposition and the country was increasingly poised for violent unrest. The working conditions and the oligarchic political system of the industrialization era, which made it difficult for workers to participate, meant that anarchism quickly gained strength among workers. [[W:Revolutionary syndicalism|Revolutionary syndicalism]] exerted a great influence on the workers' movement, especially at workers' congresses and in the strikes of the period. Anarchists also contributed to the creation of a series of periodicals for the workers' press and founded several Modern Schools around the country.
[[File:1919 open cabildo.png|left|thumb|285x285px|An {{W|open cabildo}} (a form of direct democracy involving a {{W|citizens' assembly}}) in Uxiu during the Baliscan Revolution (1917-1920)]]
The prolongation of the war in Europe helped to bring about, in 1916, an explosion of industrial production. As imported products stopped reaching Baliscan ports, national industries returned to employing workers to meet growing demand. However, there was a considerable increase in the cost of living, given that several products were exported to countries in conflict. In 1916, the prices of rice, sugar and corn increased significantly; the following year, the wholesale prices of various products went up, such as beans and [[W:Cassava|manioc flour]]; and wheat became scarce and expensive. Wages, however, remained at the same levels as in the years before the war. [[W:Food prices|Food prices]] continued to rise during the first six months of 1917. Basic items tended to be 20% to 150% more expensive than the previous year. The favorable environment for the satisfaction of demands due to the resumption of industrial activity led the labor movement to an accelerated process of reorganization in disarticulated resistance societies during the crisis, resulting in a strike wave starting in 1917.

The contentious [[1918 Cerdanyan general election]] saw the victory of Pol Pérez, a [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|notorious crony capitalist]]. His election, widely condemned as fraudulent, sparked outrage across the confederation. Left-wing activists demanded the overthrow of the aristocracy and a re-run of the election, a call that quickly spread across the nation. On 28 October 1918, over four million students, laborers, and union members took to the streets in the [[1918 Baliscan general strike]].
[[File:Decena trágica.JPG|thumb|285x285px|A group of rebel soldiers taking up positions inside the Cabildo during the events of the [[Tragic Week]]]]
The movement escalated on 21 April 1919, with the [[storming of the Baliscan Cabildo]] by rebelling soldiers supported by civilians in reaction to increasingly violent repression by the state. The successful coup against the Confederal Congress and the seizure the Cabildo [[W:Power of the deed|instigated]] the second, [[Tragic Week|more violent phase]] of the Baliscan Revolution, ultimately leading to a significant rise in {{W|political violence}} nationwide. Tensions further escalated with the assassination of Deputy Premier [[Moisès Vilchez]] by [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] [[Miquel Cebrián]]. With the country on the verge of a violent {{W|civil war}}, the Council of Ministers narrowly voted against {{W|vote of no confidence|confidence}} in Premier [[Máximo Burjassot]], forcing the administration to dissolve. Faced with continued unrest on the streets and an increasingly demoralized and hostile military, the Confederal Congress called a [[1919 Confederal Convention|Confederal Convention]].

The political revolution that followed was transformative. It resulted in the abolition of the confederal republican system and implemented {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} and {{W|Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist}} principles. Internal borders were abolished, secularization was enacted, and {{W|Socialist self-management}} took root. Araucaria’s economy, like much of Balisca’s, was transformed with significant portions placed under {{W|worker control}}. Agrarian areas were collectivized and operated as libertarian socialist communes, while social welfare programs were expanded through income and {{W|wealth redistribution}}. Socioeconomic policies were heavily influenced by the [[W:Futurism|futurist]] movement, eventually coalescing into an [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] ideology. This period also accelerated the process of {{W|indigenous decolonization}} that had begun with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]].

=== Great Depression and the Great Adonian War ===
{{Main|Estado Nuevo}}

{{See also|Balisca in the Great Adonian War}}
The post-revolution period of growth and prosperity encountered a shift as the 1930s ushered in the [[W:Great Depression|Great Depression]]. Despite the global economic turmoil, Balisca was able to to navigate this challenging time with a degree of resilience, experiencing a steady rate of endogenous (internal) growth. Like many other countries, Balisca had introduced the [[W:Gold standard|gold standard]] in the nineteenth century, meaning that it was generally possible to exchange bank notes for gold. For much of the previous period, the country had operated a [[W:Currency board|currency board]], in which a body known as the ''caja de conversión'' was charged with maintaining the value of the [[Baliscan sol|sol]] in gold. However, Balisca's experience proved distinct. While the worldwide [[W:Recession|decline]] after 1929 affected its [[Economy of Balisca|economy]] "a bit later than other countries," the depression remained "relatively mild," with [[W:Unemployment|unemployment]] peaking under 5% and no immediate [[W:Banking crisis|banking crisis]]. This resilience, despite the rigidity imposed by the gold standard, could be attributed to Balisca's economic structure. Its [[W:Worker cooperative|decentralized, worker-controlled cooperatives]] were able to implement flexible work-sharing arrangements and collective wage adjustments, thereby prioritizing job retention over mass layoffs, which significantly cushioned the impact of deflation on employment. Furthermore, the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]]'s capacity for [[W:Decentralized planning|decentralized planning]] and intervention allowed it to stimulate demand for domestically produced goods and manage essential resources outside purely market mechanisms, bypassing some of the deflationary spirals and [[W:Credit crunch|credit crunches]] that plagued [[W:Capitalism|capitalist]] economies. The absence of a [[W:Speculation|speculative]] [[W:Banking|banking sector]] also protected Balisca from the [[W:Financial contagion|financial contagion]] that amplified the depression in many gold-standard nations. This unique combination of internal economic resilience and direct state action allowed Balisca to largely counteract the typical negative effects of the gold standard during the global crisis, enabling a period of relative stability.
[[File:Concepción foundry workers.png|thumb|300x300px|[[Trade unions in Balisca|Union]] [[W:Foundry|foundry]] workers resting outside of their workplace in [[Concepción]] (1934)]]
[[File:1 Mayo Mas Que Nunca Unidad Obrera .jpg|left|thumb|419x419px|''1 Mayo Mas Que Nunca Unidad Obrera'' ("May 1st, More Than Ever Workers' Unity") poster for [[W:International Workers' Day|International Workers' Day]] (1932)]]As the global economic downturn continued, a coordinated series of initiatives were adopted across the [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]] and [[Regions of Balisca|regions]], aimed at supporting local industries and sustaining the workforce, while the confederal government implemented targeted strategies intended to stimulate demand for domestic manufacturing. Collaboration between publicly-owned cooperatives and the confederal administration contributed to stabilizing wage levels and maintaining employment, which promoted economic security and [[W:Social cohesion|social cohesion]]. Investment in infrastructure, technology, and education continued, albeit at a more gradual pace than in preceding years. The emphasis shifted from rapid expansion of transport and communication networks towards optimizing their operational efficiency and maintenance. The focus on education continued, with an emphasis on technical and vocational training to align workforce skills with economic requirements.

The confederation's adherence to communal and cantonal [[W:Autonomy|self-governance]], coupled with its decentralized industrial models, continued throughout this period. The cooperative framework provided various territorial entities and economic sectors with the capacity to navigate prevailing challenges. The expansion of [[W:Hydroelectricity|hydroelectric]] infrastructure and the enhancement of [[Rail transport in Balisca|railway networks]] continued, contributing to the confederation's stability and preparedness. Despite economic difficulties, Balisca's railroad and maritime [[W:Shipping industry|shipping industries]] continued development, though with a restrained trajectory.

In 1933, the [[Instituto por la Reconstrución Industrial]] (IRI – Institute for Industrial Reconstruction) was established to subsidize failing companies and soon controlled important portions of the national economy via {{W|government-linked companies}}. The Baliscan economy's [[W:Gross national product|gross national product]] increased 4 percent; shipping production was increased, especially that of the [[Alessi]] motor company; and the [[W:Aeronautical|aeronautical]] industry was developing. 

The [[1934 Baliscan confederal elections|1934 confederal elections]] saw increased confederal debate regarding the increasing expansionism of [[W:Empire of Japan|Japan]], [[W:Nazi Germany|Germany]] and [[W:Fascist Italy|Fascist Italy]], the failure of the [[W:League of Nations|League of Nations]] and potential intervention in the [[W:Spanish Civil War|Spanish Civil War]], and ultimately saw the continuation of the prevailing of [[W:Proletarian internationalism|pro-internationalist]] diplomatic orientation. The confederal policy however, remained [[W:Armed neutrality|armed neutrality]], though it turned a blind eye to the [[Baliscan Mafia]] and [[Internationalist Brigades]] indirectly transporting munitions and fighters to the [[W:Republican faction (Spanish Civil War)|Spanish Republican forces]]. By 1939, Balisca had welcomed around 200,000 [[W:Emigration of Jews from Nazi Germany and German-occupied Europe|Jews from Nazi Germany and German-occupied Europe]] that were not accepted into other American countries.
[[File:FEB in Aran.jpg|thumb|300x300px|Soldiers of the [[Baliscan Expeditionary Force]] disembarking in [[Aran]], 1944]]
In the immediate aftermath of the [[W:German invasion of Poland|German invasion of Poland]], public support for remaining neutral rapidly dwindled. Calls for confederal re-armament gained substantial support following the defeat of Republican forces in the {{W|Spanish Civil War}}, as it appeared very likely that Europe was on the verge of being overtaken by [[W:Fascism|fascists]]. This widespread anxiety amongst citizens and the military resulted in the calling of the [[1939 Confederal Convention]].

When Balisca refused the German and Italian demand that Balisca suspend trade relations with the Allies, Germany declared Baliscan ships would be at risk of being targeted for [[W:U-boat|U-boat]] attacks in the Atlantic. The [[W:Unrestricted submarine warfare|intensifying]] German and Italian attacks on British cargo ships was already threatening to sever shipping lanes with the United Kingdom and was rapidly eroding what little public opposition remained to [[W:Rearmament|rearmament]]. The tipping point was the German sinking of the Baliscan tanker ''Jimena'' on 2 November 1939 by the [[W:German cruiser Admiral Graf Spee|battlecruiser Graf Spee]], which prior to the encounter had been threatening British ships near Baliscan territory. The outpouring of anger that followed forced the Confederal Congress into action, and it adopted a declaration of war against Germany on 12 November, officially entering {{W|World War II}}. Following the {{W|Declaration by United Nations}}, Balisca declared war on [[W:Empire of Japan|Japan]] and {{W|Fascist Italy|Italy}}. Balisca's first engagement in the war was the [[W:Battle of the River Plate|Battle of the River Plate]].

By the end of the war, over 600,000 Baliscans had served in the armed forces, which fought primarily in the {{W|European theatre of WWII|European theatre}} and the {{W|North African campaign}}, but also participated in the efforts to liberated the [[Philippines]] post-1944. Baliscan forces played a significant role in securing shipping routes during the [[Battle of the Ethiopic]], gaining an Allied foothold in North Africa through [[W:Operation Torch|Operation Torch]], and the later the {{W|Liberation of France}} through [[W:Operation Dragoon|Operation Dragoon]] and the [[W:Italian campaign (World War II)|Italian Campaign]].

==== Economic miracle ====
{{Main|Baliscan miracle}}

=== Social revolution and internationalism ===
{{Main|Camboriú War|October 76|Special Period}}


By the beginning of the 1960s, Balisca was one of the few firmly [[W:Socialism|socialist]] in Abyala. Despite it's ideological left-wing tilt, Balisca’s reputation as one of the more reliably stable and tolerant democracies in the region made it an attractive destination for migrants, and the country deepened ties with the [[periphery countries]] in a new push for {{W|internationalism|international solidarity}}. With {{W|decolonization}} and [[W:Ethnic conflict|ethnic strife]] rapidly gaining pace around the world, Balisca opened its doors to another influx of migrants not seen since the Great Migration wave almost a century earlier. Among the new migrants were hundreds of thousands of [[Ayori diaspora|Ayori]] [[Ayori refugee crisis|displaced from their homeland]] during the [[Ayori Civil War]], and most notably [[W:Political refugees|political refugees]] arriving from other countries in the [[periphery countries]] fleeing [[W:Right-wing dictatorship#North, Central, and South America|right-wing dictatorships]] and their oppression. In the 1950s and 1960s the country enjoyed prolonged economic boom, which was accompanied by a dramatic rise in the [[W:Standard of living|standard of living]] of Baliscans. It has been calculated that the Baliscan economy experienced an average rate of growth of GDP of 5.8% per year between 1951–63, and 5.0% per year between 1964–76.[[File:Balisca_oil_disaster.png|thumb|280x280px|The [[Marénea oil disaster]] which occurred in early 1973 under suspicious circumstances, galvanized support for [[Ecological Action|radical environmentalist movements]] and the {{W|phase-out of fossil fuels}}]]
Balisca went on to develop the first [[AVB|high-speed rail network]] in the Algarbes in 1969, and the system was rapidly expanded to cope with the rising rate of urbanization occurring at the time, leading to thousands of kilometers of railways being upgraded to expand this network. {{W|Rapid transit}} and [[W:Commuter rail|commuter rail]] systems expanded nationwide to connect the main urban areas, while dams and power plants were built all over Balisca, often without regard for geological and environmental conditions. Strong urban growth led to rapid [[W:Urban sprawl|urban sprawl]] and [[W:Verticalization|verticalization]].[[File:SAW missile defense.jpg|left|thumb|280x280px|A [[W:Surface-to-air missile|surface-to-air missile]] launch system deployed in [[Porciúncula]] during hostilities with Potocsí.]]
Balisca had long been locked in territorial disputes with [[Potocsí]], with the latter rejecting Baliscan sovereignty over the [[Marañón basin]]. Balisca had asserted control over the delta during the collapse of Volisanian authority  during the [[Baliscan War of Independence]]. By 1973, the exploitation of large oil and natural gas deposits near the [[Marañón Delta]] by [[Baliscan Oil Corporation|COBAL]] had led to heightened tensions between the two countries, with Potocsí mobilizing its forces on the disputed border. This was a significant escalation of long standing tensions between the two countries, and surprised the Baliscan military, which had a minimal military presence in the territory inline with its treaties with [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|Indigenous nations]]. Tensions would continue to escalate, eventually leading to [[Camboriú War|Potoscino forces crossing the Camboriú]] in October 1976. Baliscan airspace and territorial waters were simultaneously violated by Potoscino forces in highly coordinated, multi-pronged attacks, which rapidly overwhelmed local defense forces. This was followed by an [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis|Ayori attack on Sanyan]] in November, disrupting critical oil imports to Balisca and forcing the Baliscan military to divert resources to the defense of Sanyan (pursuant to it's obligations under the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]).
[[File:October 76 standoff.jpg|thumb|Student protestors during their armed [[Blockade of Cariatiz|standoff at Cariatiz]] (1976)]]
Social unrest began with a series of [[W:Anti-authoritarianism|anti-authoritarian]] student occupation protests against increasing [[W:Elitism|elitism]] in traditional institutions, increasing [[W:Economic inequality|income disparities]] between urban and [[Rural society in Balisca|rural society]], and the increasing embrace of "[[W:Socialism from above|socialism from above]]" by the [[Revolutionary Front]]. Protests intensified after [[Secretary General of Balisca|Secretary General]] [[Mário Sá Justino]] forcefully condemned the protests as "unpatriotic" at a time of open hostilities with Potocsí, and called for the breaking up of the protests as part of the 1976 [[confederal emergency]] declaration. This led Balisca's [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade union confederations]] to call for {{W|sympathy strikes}} in support of the students, which spread far more quickly than expected to involve 14 million workers, more than 16% of the total population of Balisca at the time. The movement was rapidly characterized by spontaneous and decentralized {{W|wildcat strike action|wildcat disposition}}. The student occupations and general strikes were met with forceful attempts to quell them by university administrators, while confrontational rhetoric increased from members of the government against the student protestors. This led to many street clashes in several major cities across Balisca, and sparked divisions within the [[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous Militias]] and the [[Confederal Congress]], further complicating the confederal response to the Potoscino invasion.
[[File:People's commune kitchen.png|left|thumb|Food preparation in a [[W:People's commune#Commune life|communal kitchen]] in [[Macaio]] ({{Circa|1978}})]]
The peak of the domestic crisis coincided with the election of [[Ecological Action|ecologist]] [[Otávia Brito]] to the premiership. Under her leadership, the government encouraged and later assisted the initiative to grow their own food in any available piece of land. [[W:Urban farming|Urban gardens]] sprang up throughout the country on [[W:rooftop garden|roof-tops]], patios, and unused parking lots in raised beds as well as "[[W:Squatting|squatting]]" on empty lots. These efforts were furthered by the [[Secretariat of Local Development and Modernization]]'s efforts to teach permaculture, a sustainable agricultural system, and supporting [[W:Community gardening|communal farming]] through [[W:Agricultural cooperatives|agricultural cooperatives]], fostering a sense of [[W:Communalism|communalism]] of community engagement and capacity building. The economic depression of this "Special Period" was at its most severe between 1976 and 1978. [[W:Food insecurity|Food insecurity]] improved significantly thanks to assistance from [[Burawa]], [[Maharlika]] and members of the [[W:Comintern|Comintern]], whom together transported over? tonnes of food and other materials to Balisca during the this period. The outbreak of war and the failure of [[Breisland]] and [[Volisania]] to condemn the Potoscino invasion triggered an increase in anti-Westernism across the country, and effectively froze any plans for deepening relations.

Changes during the Special Period included an unprecedented level of responsibility and cooperation at the confederal level by the communes and regions, and internationally, the diplomatic realignment of the country towards the Soviet Union. This period radically transformed Baliscan society and the economy, as it necessitated the revival of [[Rural society in Balisca|rural Balisca]], hastened the shift to [[W:Organic agriculture|organic agriculture]], curtailed meat production and consumption, stunted the [[W:Car dependency|growth of car ownership]] in the country as fuel was prioritized for diesel cargo trains and electricity, overhauled industry, health, and diets nationwide. The Camboriú War also prompted a significant reevaluation of Baliscan military policy, with the decentralized structure of the [[Confederal Armed Forces|armed forces]] blamed for inefficiency and the failure to precipitate the conflict. It's around this time that Balisca accelerated its moves to decouple from it's cozy position with the [[W:Western world|Western world]] to a more [[W:Anti-imperialism|anti-imperialist]] and anti-colonial position. Balisca would go on to negotiate the [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement]] with [[Burawa]], [[Maharlika]] and Sanyan.

In January 1977 the Baliscan government accepted international calls for a ceasefire, but relations with Potocsí remained severed, in part due to the ongoing devastation of the [[Marañón oil fires]]. The two countries later signed the [[Kuluba Accords]] in [[Kuluba]], [[Asikyira]] in 1982, in which Potocsí agreed to recognize Baliscan sovereignty over the delta in exchange for the establishment of a [[W:Demilitarized zone|demilitarized zone]] (DMZ) covering the [[Marañón basin]]. Relations between the two countries were not normalized in 1985.

=== Post–Cold War years ===
{{Main|Post–Cold War era}}

[[File:Izamiento.jpg|left|thumb|340x340px|The first flag raising ceremony at the headquarters of [[W:Mercosur|Mercosur]] in [[W:Montevideo|Montevideo]] (2001)]]
The [[W:Revolutions of 1989|dramatic collapse]] of the [[W:Eastern Bloc|Eastern Bloc]] and the [[Soviet Reformation|partial collapse]] of the [[Soviet Union]] in the late 1980s and early 1990s presented Balisca with a new and significant geopolitical challenge, but also, paradoxically, an ideological vindication. Ideologically, for Balisca's leadership and populace, the collapse of the Eastern Bloc was interpreted not as a failure of socialism itself, but as a direct consequence of [[W:Authoritarian socialism|authoritarian]], [[W:State socialism|centralized governance]] that was typical of the Soviet Union and it's satellite states. While the external events strengthened faith in Balisca's ideological path, they also sparked internal debates about the country's economic resilience and vulnerability in a rapidly globalizing, increasingly neoliberal world. Discussions largely revolved around the view of many that Balisca would have to find the optimal balance between self-sufficiency and [[W:Market socialism|strategic engagement]] with global markets, in order to ensure that economic interactions did not compromise their core principles of [[W:Social ownership|worker's control over the economy]]. 

In a significant pragmatic move to secure new regional economic partnerships following the Eastern Bloc's dissolution and reinforce its South American alignment, Balisca sought and gained membership in [[W:Mercosur|Mercosur]]''',''' the emerging South American common market, during this period. This decision, while posing challenges for integrating Balisca's unique worker-controlled enterprises into a broader capitalist customs union, was sold domestically as a strategic step to foster regional economic resilience and counterbalance to increasingly dominant globalizing pressures, rather than a full embrace of neoliberalism. Additionally, the emergence of the United States as the sole global superpower and the [[W:Global capitalism|ascendance of neoliberal economic policies]] presented new pressures on Balisca's distinct socio-economic model, and the shifting of [[Balisca-United States relations]] during this time can be attributed to the consistent and vocal anti-imperialist rhetoric from Baliscan politicians in response to American military interventions and policies across the emerging [[W:Unipolar world|unipolar world]].

===Recent===
{{Main|2021 Baliscan civil unrest}}

{{See also|Baliscan duo}}
During the tenure of [[Cristiano Sandoval]] the Confederal Congress ratified the [[2008 Ecology Initiative]], condemned the [[W:Iraq War|American-led invasion of Iraq]] and [[W:War on Terror|War on Terror]], and launched an international campaign in support of [[W:Drug liberalization|drug liberalization]] against the destructive [[W:War on Drugs|War on Drugs]]. His administration redoubled Balisca's efforts to achieve {{W|Reform of the UNSC|reform of the UNSC}} (especially regarding a permanent seat for itself). Balisca expanded its ties to {{W|Africa}}, signed the {{W|Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons}}, and entered Balisca into the [[Okami Protocol]]. Additionally, Sandoval oversaw the establishment of the second overseas Baliscan military outpost in [[Camp Amana|Zambezi]].

Sandoval was succeeded by [[Andrea Palacios]] in 2019 following her victory in the [[2018 Baliscan premiership election]]. Her election was largely viewed as an [[W:Upset (competition)|electoral upset]] against the [[W:Left-wing nationalism|left-wing nationalist]] rhetoric of her primary opponent [[Josuè Subirà]]. Palacios was elected by a margin of 62,784 votes (0.08%), the narrowest result in Baliscan history. Palacios' tenure was plagued by congressional setbacks and gridlock, and by the end of her first year in office her approval rating had fallen to 33%. Her reformist-oriented program caused significant fighting between the liberal [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]] and the {{W|anti-establishment}} [[Cosmopolitan Collective]], resulting in significant portions of her program never being enacted. She held office through the first waves of the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}} and the resulting COVID-19 recession starting in 2020, during which her proactive response gathered widespread praise.
[[File:2021_Baliscan_riots.jpg|thumb|Two students with [[W:molotov cocktails|molotov cocktails]] looks on during {{W|protest}}s against {{W|economic inequality}} and {{W|democracy deficit}} in [[Merced]] during the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]].]]
Palacios avoided becoming embroiled in the crisis surrounding [[Balausia]]'s detention of the [[Baliscan duo]], instead delegating the task to Deputy Secretary General [[Zavier Bilal]]. His response to the dispute was widely praised by the Baliscan public, leading to significant rises in his approval ratings. However Bilal and several other secretaries later broke with Palacios following her personal intervention to accept the proposed [[Portvell Agreement]] between Baliscan and Balausian negotiators regarding the fate of the Baliscan duo. Bilal would later break with the government position and align himself with the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|Indignados movement]]. After an online petition posted in June had attracted nearly 4 million signatures, mass demonstrations began on 11 July.

The movement was initially motivated by the [[Baliscan duo|execution of Silvestre Santmartí]], increasing [[Baliscan Social Living Index|cost of living]], and [[W:Economic inequality|economic inequality]]; it asserted that the confederal government was no longer working for the well being of the public. On 13 July, a list of 50 demands was made public and went viral on social media, becoming de facto a structuring basis for the movement, covering a wide range of eclectic topics, mostly related to democracy, social and fiscal justice. The protesters successfully pushed for {{W|Economic liberalization|the cancellation of market liberalization reforms}}, deeper confederal and [[W:Direct democracy|direct democratic]] reform, {{W|climate justice}} and [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration reform]], among other things.

Violence sporadically occurred predominantly between anti-statist protesters, including [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchists]], [[W:Social ecology (Bookchin)|social ecologists]] and [[Accelerationism|accelerationists]]; and pro-reformist, pro-statist, and right-wing activists. In [[Castejón]], protests progressively became more violent and multifaceted, ultimately leading the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|metropolitan council]] to impose a [[W:state of emergency|state of emergency]] to focus on combating the growing unrest. Imminently facing a [[W:Recall vote|recall vote]], Secretary General Palacios made a televised address on 27 July announcing her resignation from political office. With this resignation the movement's demands evolved; a transition towards political {{W|decentralization}} away from Castejón and the [[Government and politics of Balisca|confederal government]], along with {{W|participatory democracy|participatory}} and {{W|grassroots democracy}} became its unique revendication. On the same day, anarchists utilizing fireworks and [[W:Improvised weapon|improvised weapons]] in Castejón's [[Capitoline Hill]] forced the local [[Mozos de Escuadra]] to retreat, and declared the first [[Autonomous commune (Balisca)|autonomous commune]].

Deputy Secretary General Bilal assumed the duties as SecGen immediately after Andrea Palacios' resignation. Sizable portions of the Baliscan public, especially from the right wing, and some members of the international community alike called for the next confederal elections to be moved ahead; they ultimately [[2021 Baliscan confederal election|occurred in October]]. As a result of the elections, the {{W|neoliberal}} [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]] lost all of it's seats, while the number {{W|moderate}} and {{W|conservative}} delegates elected was significantly reduced, winning elections in only 23 [[Commune district|commune districts]] (out of 736), and the electorate rallying around {{W|anti-capitalist}}, {{W|anti-statism|anti-statist}} and [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] candidates. It was under Bilal's government that the [[2022 Baliscan constitutional referendum|referendum to approve the new Constitution]] was held on 23 July 2022, which passed with the support of 69% of the electorate.

Under Bilal's leadership, Balisca has shifted to the forefront of international efforts supporting [[W:Eco-socialism|eco-socialism]], [[W:Social justice|social justice]], [[W:Alter-globalization|alter-globalization]], and [[W:Global justice movement|global justice]], signaling Balisca's "[[Baliscan accelerationism|acceleration]]" towards a {{W|Postnationalism|postnationalist}} and {{W|Cosmopolitanism|cosmopolitan}} society. It has additionally cultivated closer ties with countries such as [[Burawa]], [[Daras]] and [[Okaiken]]. This policy has been criticized for straining relations with countries that Balisca has typically had cordial ties, but praised for the expansion of [[Concepción Organization of Cooperation|COCO]] and it's advocacy for {{W|alter-globalization}} and opposition to [[W:Neo-nationalism|neo-nationalism]].


